WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.960
Safe file handling in Java starts with
inventory.

2
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.496
Find the places where the application
reads, writes, moves, deletes,

3
00:00:06.576 --> 00:00:10.383
streams, archives, extracts, generates,
transforms, uploads, downloads,

4
00:00:10.463 --> 00:00:11.342
and serves files.

5
00:00:11.422 --> 00:00:14.292
Classify file types by business purpose
and sensitivity.

6
00:00:14.372 --> 00:00:17.535
Separate public assets, private files,
temporary files, logs,

7
00:00:17.615 --> 00:00:20.427
configuration, backups, generated files,
and secrets.

8
00:00:20.507 --> 00:00:24.490
A clear map makes it much easier to see
which paths and operations deserve tighter

9
00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:25.097
controls.

10
00:00:25.177 --> 00:00:28.515
Avoid passing user-controlled paths
directly to filesystem APIs.

11
00:00:28.595 --> 00:00:32.813
Use fixed storage boundaries and verify
resolved paths stay inside them when dynamic

12
00:00:32.893 --> 00:00:34.064
selection is required.

13
00:00:34.144 --> 00:00:37.424
Prefer application-controlled IDs and
generated storage names.

14
00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:39.964
Treat original filenames as untrusted
metadata.

15
00:00:40.044 --> 00:00:43.676
Use allowlists and multiple validation
signals for file type decisions.

16
00:00:43.756 --> 00:00:47.915
Pair path checks with object-level
authorization so a caller cannot access another

17
00:00:47.995 --> 00:00:51.685
user's or tenant's file even when the path
stays inside the base directory.

18
00:00:51.765 --> 00:00:54.928
Run with least privilege and avoid broad
writable directories.

19
00:00:55.008 --> 00:00:59.050
Keep application code read-only where
possible and place runtime data in narrow,

20
00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:00.360
intentional locations.

21
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:04.188
Use streaming, limits, timeouts, quotas,
and cleanup for resource safety.

22
00:01:04.268 --> 00:01:07.959
Handle temporary files, partial writes,
archive extraction, downloads,

23
00:01:08.039 --> 00:01:11.436
and generated exports as full workflows
with safe failure behavior.

24
00:01:11.516 --> 00:01:15.557
Enforce authorization before private
downloads, previews, generated exports,

25
00:01:15.637 --> 00:01:16.692
and metadata access.

26
00:01:16.772 --> 00:01:20.052
A practical first-week plan is direct:
find all filesystem calls,

27
00:01:20.132 --> 00:01:22.768
map storage directories, review path
construction,

28
00:01:22.848 --> 00:01:26.831
check public and private storage
separation, confirm download authorization,

29
00:01:26.911 --> 00:01:30.718
add size limits, review temporary-file
cleanup, inspect archive handling,

30
00:01:30.798 --> 00:01:32.380
remove sensitive path logging,

31
00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.388
and add tests for traversal and
authorization boundaries.

32
00:01:35.468 --> 00:01:39.100
Then build file-handling review into pull
requests, CI/CD, monitoring,

33
00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:42.987
logging, runbooks, and incident response
so the controls stay current as the

34
00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:44.180
application evolves.
